## 2. Change-Related Testing (Regression, Smoke, Sanity)

Whenever developers modify or update code, QA must run specific tests to ensure nothing was damaged.

### Regression Testing

* *What it is:* Testing the entire existing application after a code change to ensure that new features or bug fixes didn't accidentally break old features that were previously working.
* *Real-world Example:* A developer updates the "Change Password" feature. You run *Regression Testing* on the "Add to Cart" and "Checkout" features to make sure the developer's changes didn't accidentally break the shopping system. This is why automation tools like Playwright or Selenium are heavily used—running these tests manually every time code changes is exhausting.

### Smoke Testing

* *What it is:* A quick, basic suite of tests run immediately after a new software build is deployed. It checks if the most critical, core functionalities work. If the smoke test fails, the build is rejected.
* *Origin:* The term comes from hardware engineering—plugging a device in to see if "smoke" comes out of it. If it smokes, turn it off.
* *Software Example:* Checking if the app launches, if a user can log in, and if the main homepage loads. If you can't log in, there's no point in running any other detailed tests.

### Sanity Testing

* *What it is:* A quick, highly targeted test executed after receiving a software build with a minor bug fix. It proves that the specific feature works and the bug is actually gone.
* *Difference from Smoke:* Smoke testing is broad but shallow (testing the whole app's health). Sanity testing is narrow but deep (focusing strictly on the component that was just fixed).

---

## 3. The Functional Hierarchy (The Testing Levels)

When building an application, software is tested progressively from small pieces up to the entire completed system.

* *Unit Testing:* Testing the smallest individual piece of code in isolation (like a single Python function). This is usually written and executed by developers.
* *Integration Testing:* Testing how two or more individual pieces or modules interact with each other (e.g., making sure your requests API code can successfully write data to a SQL database module).
* *System Testing:* Testing the entire, fully integrated application from end-to-end to ensure it matches the business requirements (this is where QA teams spend most of their time).
* *User Acceptance Testing (UAT):* The final phase before release. Real users or clients test the application to ensure it handles actual business tasks to their satisfaction before signing off.

