- Black-Box Testing: Testing the system purely from the outside via the UI or API without knowing or seeing the internal code structure. (This is where most QA engineers operate).
- White-Box Testing: Testing the application with full visibility into the internal code, logic paths, loops, and statement branches. (e.g., Code coverage metrics, Unit testing)
- Gray-Box Testing: A hybrid approach. You test from the outside like a black-box tester, but you use structural knowledge of the internal code or databases to design sharper, 
high-risk test cases (e.g., viewing log tables or API payloads while navigating the UI).

-Exploratory Testing : Simultaneous learning, test design, and test execution. 
Instead of following rigid, pre-written step-by-step test scripts, you actively explore the application like a real, unpredictable user to hunt for unscripted edge cases.

